const title = 'How to get an apostille in New York';
const description = 'New York apostille and certificate of authentication: the official $10 fee, Department of State offices, same-day walk-in limits, and mail instructions, from the official DOS page.';
---
<Base title={title} description={description} updated="2026-11-22">
  <h1>How to get an apostille in New York</h1>
  <div class="answer"><p><strong>Short answer:</strong> New York apostilles come from the Department of State's Apostille and Authentication Unit and cost $10 per document. Notarized papers and most county-issued vital records need a county clerk certification first. Mail the request to Albany, or use same-day walk-in service in New York City, Albany, Binghamton, Buffalo or Utica (up to 10 documents). <Src href="https://dos.ny.gov/apostille-certificate-authentication" /></p></div>
  <p class="lede">The New York Department of State authenticates public documents issued in New York State that are signed by a New York State official or county clerk. The same office issues either an apostille or a certificate of authentication, depending on the destination country.</p>

  <h2>At a glance</h2>
  <div class="tablewrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Item</th><th>What the official page says</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Issuing office</td><td>New York Department of State, Division of Licensing Services, Apostille and Authentication Unit<Src href="https://dos.ny.gov/apostille-certificate-authentication" /></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Fee</td><td>$10 per document<Src href="https://dos.ny.gov/apostille-certificate-authentication" /></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Mail</td><td>Yes. Processed according to receipt date. Returned by first-class mail unless you enclose a prepaid overnight shipping label. The office does not accept labels that bill your credit card or “bill sender.”</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Mailing address</td><td>New York Department of State, Division of Licensing Services, Apostille and Authentication Unit, PO Box 22001, Albany, NY 12201-2001. Express and next-day carriers should use the Albany physical address: 1 Commerce Plaza, 99 Washington Avenue, 6th Floor, Albany, NY 12231.<Src href="https://dos.ny.gov/apostille-certificate-authentication" /></td></tr>
        <tr><td>In person</td><td>Same-day walk-in service in New York City, Albany, Binghamton, Buffalo, and Utica. Limited to 10 documents per customer. Drop-off (not treated as a priority; processed by receipt date) is allowed only in Albany and New York City.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Mail processing time</td><td>—</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>The official page does not publish a routine turnaround time for mailed or drop-off requests. We therefore show a dash rather than estimate one.<Src href="https://dos.ny.gov/apostille-certificate-authentication" /></p>

  <h2>Where can you get a same-day apostille in New York?</h2>
  <p>Walk-in same-day apostille and authentication services, as published:<Src href="https://dos.ny.gov/apostille-certificate-authentication" /></p>
  <ul>
    <li>New York City, 123 William St., 2nd Fl., New York, NY 10038-3804, 9:00 a.m. to 4:00 p.m.</li>
    <li>Albany, 1 Commerce Plaza, 99 Washington Avenue, 6th Floor, Albany, NY 12231, 9:00 a.m. to 4:15 p.m.</li>
    <li>Binghamton, State Office Building, 44 Hawley Street, Room 1506, Binghamton, NY 13901-4455, 9:00 a.m. to 12:00 p.m. and 12:45 p.m. to 4:00 p.m.</li>
    <li>Buffalo, 295 Main Street, 8th Floor, Suite 821, Buffalo, NY 14203, 9:00 a.m. to 12:00 p.m. and 12:45 p.m. to 4:00 p.m.</li>
    <li>Utica, State Office Building, 207 Genesee Street, Utica, NY 13501-2812, 9:00 a.m. to 12:00 p.m. and 12:45 p.m. to 4:00 p.m.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The Department of State call center is (518) 474-4429, 8:45 a.m. to 4:30 p.m. Monday through Friday except legal holidays.<Src href="https://dos.ny.gov/apostille-certificate-authentication" /></p>
  <p>Credit-card payments on mailed requests require a CVV on the current request form; the office says older forms without a CVV can cause the payment to be rejected and the documents returned.<Src href="https://dos.ny.gov/apostille-certificate-authentication" /></p>

  <h2>What has to happen before you file?</h2>
  <p>The Department of State only authenticates public documents issued in New York State and signed by a New York State official or county clerk.<Src href="https://dos.ny.gov/apostille-certificate-authentication" /></p>
  <ul>
    <li>Birth, death, and marriage certificates and certificates of divorce issued by a local or county official, or a county department of health, must first be certified by the county clerk of the county where the document was issued.</li>
    <li>Vital records issued by the New York State Department of Health and bearing the signature of the Director of Vital Statistics or the NYS Registrar may be submitted without a county-clerk certification. The official page warns that the words “New York State,” “registrar,” or “vital records” on a local certificate do not, by themselves, mean it was issued by the State Department of Health.</li>
    <li>Notarized documents must be certified by the county clerk of the county where the notary is commissioned.</li>
    <li>A NYS criminal background check must be submitted with the cover letter bearing the signature of the Division of Criminal Justice Services representative. Federal/FBI background checks must be authenticated by the U.S. Department of State.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Do you need an apostille or a certificate of authentication?</h2>
  <p>An apostille is issued under the 1961 Hague Convention for use in countries that have joined the Convention. A Certificate of Authentication is issued for use in a country that is not a member. The official page says Certificates of Authentication “may also require an additional certification from the United States Department of State prior to submission to the foreign country.”<Src href="https://dos.ny.gov/apostille-certificate-authentication" /></p>
  <p>Both certificates are described as a one-page document with a blue laser-printed facsimile of the Department of State seal and the facsimile signature of the Secretary of State or a deputy. Apostilles and certificates issued on or after April 9, 2013, may be verified through the Department’s online tool, linked from the official page.<Src href="https://dos.ny.gov/apostille-certificate-authentication" /></p>

